# mcpfold

> Open-source, local-first MCP config manager. Keep one canonical `mcp.config.jsonc` and fold
> it out to every MCP client — Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Cursor, VS Code, Windsurf, Zed,
> Cline, and Gemini CLI. mcpfold curates which servers and tools each client loads to cut
> context-window usage (~80% fewer tool-schema tokens in a reproducible benchmark), and stores
> secrets as `${provider:path}` references, never as values.

mcpfold solves two problems: MCP config sprawls across clients that use divergent formats
(VS Code uses the root key `servers`, Zed uses `context_servers`, everyone else uses
`mcpServers`), and every connected server dumps its full tool schema into the model's context
window on every turn. mcpfold keeps one source of truth, folds it out per client, and trims
each client's toolset via a local proxy. The core engine and CLI are MIT-licensed.

Quick start: `npx mcpfold init` → `mcpfold import` → `mcpfold sync`. Check drift with
`mcpfold diff`.

## Docs
- [Quickstart](https://mcpfold.com/docs/quickstart): install, init, import, sync in under a minute.
- [Config format](https://mcpfold.com/docs/config-format): the canonical `mcp.config.jsonc` schema.
- [Adapters / clients](https://mcpfold.com/docs/coverage): supported clients and their formats.
- [Benchmark](https://mcpfold.com/docs/benchmark): how the ~80% context reduction is measured.

